---
title: "CLI reference"
description: "Every subcommand and flag: preview, build and publish, the check gate, and the machine-facing read, map, vocab, mcp and lsp."
---

*Reference: CLI · [Shell completion](shell-completion.tmd) · [Front matter](frontmatter.tmd) · [Cell options](cell-options.tmd) · [Configuration](configuration.tmd) · [Shortcodes](shortcodes.tmd) · [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.tmd) · [Accessibility](accessibility.tmd)*

| Command | Effect |
|---|---|
| `preview <file.tmd> [port]` | Live preview server (default port 4321; aliases `dev`, `serve`) |
| `preview <dir>` | Live **multi-page site/book** preview (cross-page nav + per-page hot reload) |
| `build <file.tmd> [out.html]` | Write a self-contained HTML file (default `<name>.html`) |
| `build <file.tmd> --out <dir>` | Write `<dir>/index.html` + copied assets (a portable folder) |
| `build <file.tmd> --bare` | Write a **bare** page: zero `<script>`, zero CDN, CSS-only theme (drafts / archives / print input) |
| `build <dir> [--out <dir>]` | Build a **multi-page site/book** to `_site/` / `_book/` |
| `publish <dir> [--project-name <name>] [--public] [--no-strict] [--dry-run]` | Build a site/book + deploy it to Cloudflare Pages behind a shared passcode by default (`--public` opts out); strict by default (`--no-strict` opts out); Wrangler direct upload |
| `render <file.tmd>` | Render a one-shot full HTML page to stdout (static; does **not** execute code cells) |
| `read <file.tmd> [--run]` | Project the document to plain text (agent-readable). Static by default; `--run` executes `{python}`/`{r}` cells and, when a local headless Chrome is available, observes each `{js}` chart (`[js: produced, <svg W×H>]`), reporting what every cell produced (`--format json` for structured per-cell results) |
| `blocks <file.tmd>` | List block ids and sourcepos (for debugging) |
| `schema [--out <dir>]` | Emit the bundled JSON Schemas for `_site.yml` + front matter (editor autocomplete) |
| `vocab` | Emit the editor autocomplete vocabulary as JSON (the companion consumes it) |
| `check <file.tmd\|dir> [--format human\|json] [--errors-only\|--strict]` | List every located diagnostic and exit non-zero on any error or warning (a CI / pre-publish gate) |
| `init [dir]` | Scaffold a previewable project in `dir` (default `.`): `_site.yml`, `index.tmd`, `AGENTS.md`, and a `.taliesin/` holding the editor JSON schemas. `--template site\|book` adds that starter's pages; refuses to overwrite |
| `new <post\|page\|deck\|paper> <slug>` | Scaffold one document, correct on its first save; refuses to overwrite (`paper` is citation-wired: `bibliography:` + a `[@key]` + a shipped `references.bib`) |
| `symbols <file.tmd> [--format human\|json]` | List the document's cross-reference targets (what you can write after `@`) |
| `map <dir> [--format human\|json]` | Whole-project outline: pages, nav, drafts, xref graph, mounts (agent planning) |
| `mcp` | Run a stdio **MCP server** exposing check/read/symbols/map/vocab/build tools |
| `lsp` | Run a stdio **LSP server** for live `.tmd` diagnostics in any LSP editor (Neovim, Helix, Zed, VS Code) |
| `completions <bash\|zsh\|fish>` | Print a shell completion script to stdout (command names + file arguments) |

`check` renders the document or site in memory and reports everything that flows
through the diagnostics channel: unknown front-matter / `_site.yml` / cell-option /
container keys (with did-you-mean), unknown callout kinds, broken `@xref`
cross-references, unknown shortcodes, a missing `bibliography:`, duplicate heading
`{#id}`s, broken in-page `#anchor` links, and opt-in `prose-lint:` findings. It also
catches these **publish-blocking** mistakes:

| Rule | Flags |
|---|---|
| Broken relative link | A `[text](other.tmd)` / `[text](page.html#x)` whose **local target file** is missing. In a site, links resolve against the page registry (`.tmd`→`.html`), so a cross-page anchor `page.html#frag` whose `frag` is no id on that page is flagged too. **External `http(s)://` links are never checked**: `check` stays offline and deterministic, and a `{{< embed >}}` deck or a cross-book / mounted link (one that climbs above the site root) is left alone. |
| Missing local video | A `{{< video clip.mp4 >}}` (or a raw `<video src>` / `<video><source></video>`) whose local file is absent (the video sibling of the local-image check). `<audio>` sources are skipped (often streamed/generated). |
| Dangling reactive input | A `{js}` cell's `//\| input: x` that no `{js}` cell (`//\| name`/`//\| viewof`) or `{{< input >}}` control defines, with a did-you-mean over the known names. Suppressed when the doc has a Python/R cell (a runtime `define` may publish the name). |
| Reactive dependency cycle | A loop among `{js}` cells (`a` consumes `b`, `b` consumes `a`), statically mirroring the runtime graph so none would run. |
| Heading-level skip | A heading that jumps two or more levels deeper than the one before it (an `h2` directly followed by an `h4`, skipping `h3`), which breaks the document outline for screen readers. Only a true *mid-document* skip is flagged (never "doesn't start at `h1`"), and slide decks are skipped (they are slide-structured). The static half of the live preview's a11y audit. |
| Missing alt text | A hand-written / passthrough `<img>` with no `alt` attribute, so a screen reader can't describe it (a decorative image should set `alt=""`). Markdown `![…](…)` always emits an `alt`, so this catches raw `<img>` only. |
| No accessible name | An `<a href>` / `<button>` in content with empty text and no `aria-label` / `title` / labelled image (e.g. an icon-only link), which a screen reader announces as just "link" / "button". |

It does **not** execute code cells (it is fast, kernel-free,
and deterministic, so runtime cell errors stay in `preview`/`build`). Output is a
greppable `path:line: severity[CODE]: message` linter line by default, closed by a
per-severity summary (`3 problems (1 error, 2 warnings)`) and a `taliesin check
--explain <CODE>` footer; or a `{ "diagnostics": [{file, line, message, code, severity,
docs_url}], "environment": [...] }` object with `--format json`. It exits `0` when
nothing above the severity floor fired, so it drops straight into CI or a pre-commit hook:

```sh
taliesin check .                          # fails (exit 1) on any error or warning
taliesin check . --strict                 # …and on suggestions too
taliesin check . --errors-only            # …on errors alone; warnings neither shown nor gated
taliesin check . --format json > problems.json
```

## What fails the run

There are three severities, and they exist to keep the gate honest: advice about how a
sentence reads must not have the same power over CI as a broken cross-reference.

| Severity | Means | Fails `check` |
|---|---|---|
| `error` | The document is wrong: a broken ref, a dead link, unparseable YAML | always |
| `warning` | Something will silently not work: an unknown key, a missing `alt` | by default; not under `--errors-only` |
| `suggestion` | Advice you may decline: the opt-in `prose-lint:` findings | never, unless you pass `--strict` |

A suggestion is always **printed**; only the exit code changes. `build --strict` and
`publish` (strict by default) use the same rule, so a style suggestion can never block a
release, and the summary says so rather than reporting advice as a problem beside an
exit `0`:

```
doc.tmd:6: suggestion[TAL-PROSE-WEASEL]: weasel word `simply` (consider cutting)
1 suggestion (advice; nothing here fails the run)
```

`--format json` stays valid JSON even when `check` itself can't run: an unreadable or
missing path (or an empty site) is reported as a single `{"error": "…"}` object on
**stdout** (with a non-zero exit), so `taliesin check x --format json | jq` never chokes
on a stray human message. Human format keeps that error on stderr.

`check` is a static linter, so it does **not** spawn interpreters — the environment audit
lives in `taliesin doctor`. It does, though, tell you **which** interpreter it would use.
Whenever the target contains `{python}` or `{r}` cells, the human output ends with a
footer naming the resolved interpreter per language:

```
no problems found

Environment (not probed):
  python: /home/you/.venv/bin/python (project .venv)
run `taliesin doctor` to check these kernels are ready
```

Naming it costs nothing and answers the question a silent pass could not: a document whose
only cell cannot run used to report `no problems found` and exit 0, while `build` on the
same file warned twice. The footer deliberately carries **no verdict** — nothing spawned
that binary, so `check` does not claim to know whether it works. A wrong `TALIESIN_PYTHON`
or a stale `.venv` is visible at a glance; whether the kernel is ready is `doctor`'s answer.

Two paths do probe. `--format json` always carries the full `environment` array (for each
language used: the resolved interpreter, where it came from, and whether its `ipykernel` /
`IRkernel` package is importable), because agents want it; and `--require-kernel` probes to
gate on it. Under `--require-kernel` the human output replaces the footer above with only
the **degraded** languages (an all-green probe is `doctor`'s business) and points at
`taliesin doctor` for the full audit. See the [configuration
reference](configuration.html) for how the interpreter is chosen.

A site's `{{< embed >}}`-referenced **decks** count toward this footer too. A deck is built
and served but held out of the page list, so a project whose only code cells live in a talk
would otherwise report no environment at all.

**A project-supplied interpreter is reported, not run.** When the interpreter comes from
the project being checked — a `_site.yml` `python:` / `r:` field, or the project's own
`.venv` — `check` names it but never spawns it, and its `runs` is `null` with a
`not_probed` reason. Spawning a binary a *document's* author named is execution, and
`check` is the pass you run on a project before you have read it. `--require-kernel` (or
`taliesin doctor`) is the opt-in that probes it. An interpreter you chose yourself
(`TALIESIN_PYTHON` / `TALIESIN_R`, or the `python3` on your `PATH`) is probed as before.

At a glance, here is everything `check` looks at, grouped by what it protects:

| Category | Catches |
|---|---|
| **Config typos** | Unknown front-matter, `_site.yml`, cell-option, and `:::` container keys (with did-you-mean), unknown callout kinds, unknown shortcode names **and their flags/arguments** (`{{< video x.mp4 control >}}` suggests `controls`), YAML parse errors |
| **Cross-references** | Broken `@fig-` / `@sec-` / `@eq-` / `@lst-` / `@tbl-` refs (no matching label), a citation with no `bibliography:` |
| **Links & anchors** | Broken relative `.tmd`/`.html` links (and, in a site, cross-page links + cross-page `#anchor`s), broken in-page `#anchor`s, duplicate heading `{#id}`s |
| **Local media** | Missing local images (`src`/`href`) and `{{< video >}}`/`<video>` files |
| **Reactive `{js}`** | A `//\| input:` naming nothing that's defined, a dependency cycle among `{js}` cells |
| **Accessibility** | An `<img>` with no `alt`, a mid-document heading-level skip (decks exempt), an `<a>`/`<button>` with no accessible name |
| **Opt-in** | `prose-lint:` findings (weasel words, repeated words, your own banned list) — all severity `suggestion` |

External `http(s)://` links are never fetched (`check` stays offline and
deterministic), and it never executes code cells. On its own, `check` exits `1` if it
finds an error or a warning in any of those categories; it is the kernel-free gate.

`--strict` also exists on **`build`**, where it means the same thing one step further
along: it promotes a normally non-fatal build into a hard failure, so the page is still
written but `build` exits non-zero if **any located warning** fired or **any code cell
errored** at runtime. Advice is exempt there too.
Because `build` does run cells, `--strict` adds the one thing `check` structurally
can't see (a cell that *throws*) on top of the located diagnostics, which makes
`taliesin check . && taliesin build . --strict` a thorough two-stage CI gate: `check`
fails fast and kernel-free on structure, then a strict `build` proves every cell
actually runs.

`--version` / `-V` prints the version followed by the git commit it was built from,
in parentheses:

```text
Taliesin 0.2.0 (cdf0a13)
```

`--help` / `-h` (or no arguments) prints the
top-level usage. Putting `--help` / `-h` **after** a subcommand
(`taliesin build --help`, `taliesin check -h`, …) prints a focused page for just that
command: its synopsis, its flags, and a one-line example. If
the default port (or one you pass) is already in use, what happens depends on who holds
it. A preview of the *same* project is replaced, so re-running `preview` hands back the
canonical URL instead of stacking a second server (every extra one would keep its own
file watcher and kernel re-executing the same sources, on a port nobody is watching). A
port held by anything else falls back to the next free port. Either way the chosen URL
is logged.

`preview` takes these flags (they may appear anywhere on the line):

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--host` | Bind your LAN and print a **QR code** for phones, gated by a per-session access token (see below) |
| `--open` | Launch the default browser at the preview URL |
| `--no-exec` | Render code cells as source without executing them: no kernel is started, and a `{js}` cell is not handed to the browser either. It does **not** strip raw HTML — see below |

### Documents you did not write

Previewing a `.tmd` runs it. That is the point of the tool, and it means **opening a
document someone sent you is the same kind of decision as running a script they sent
you.** Taliesin is honest about this rather than pretending to a sandbox it does not have
(see also [`SECURITY.md`](https://github.com/AJBogo9/taliesin/blob/main/SECURITY.md)):

- **Code cells execute.** `{python}` / `{r}` run against a Jupyter kernel with your
  permissions; a `{js}` cell runs in your browser on the preview's origin.
- **Raw HTML passes through**, because Markdown says it does. So does anything a
  document injects via `include-in-header`, `include-before-body`, `include-after-body`
  or `css:` — those files are inserted verbatim.
- **`--no-exec` stops the code cells** — both kinds — and starts no kernel. It is *not* a
  sanitizer: raw `<script>` in the document body and the `include-*` / `css:` injections
  still reach the page. There is deliberately no HTML sanitizer and no CSP, because a
  sanitizer that a document's own author can work around buys a false sense of safety.
- **Two things are enforced rather than documented**, because they are the tool being
  steered *outside* the document by its metadata rather than the document running its own
  code: a `mounts:` path may not leave the directory beside the project, and `check` never
  spawns an interpreter the project itself named.

The practical reading: `--no-exec` is the right flag for *skimming* a stranger's document,
and a container or a throwaway user is the right answer for *trusting* one.

`build` takes these flags (they may appear anywhere on the line):

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--out <dir>` | Write a portable `<dir>/index.html` + copied assets instead of a single file |
| `--strict` | Exit non-zero if any cell errored or any located warning fired (a CI gate; the page is still written) |
| `--bare` | Single-doc only: emit a **bare** page (zero `<script>`, zero CDN, CSS-only theming) |
| `--jobs <N>` (alias `-j`) | Maximum pages to build in parallel (default: auto, memory- and core-capped; `--jobs 1` forces sequential; `--jobs 0` same as auto) |

A regular `build` already content-gates the optional enhancer scripts (mermaid, `{js}`,
walkthrough, tabset, scrolly) to the constructs a page actually uses, so a prose page
ships less JavaScript without losing any feature. `--bare` goes further for a
single document: it ships **no** `<script>` at all and themes purely with CSS (a forced
`theme:` is hard-coded; otherwise it follows the OS via `prefers-color-scheme`).
Server-rendered math still works; `{js}` cells, Mermaid rendering, and the
copy/lightbox/reader controls are dropped (each warned, never silently). A slide deck or
a `<dir>` site is refused (their navigation needs JavaScript). A `build` skips code
execution only through the `TALIESIN_NO_EXEC=1` environment variable, not a `--no-exec`
flag.

A `--bare` page is still **responsive**: the viewport meta tag and every CSS media
query ship as usual, so it reflows on a phone and respects `prefers-color-scheme`. It
just has no behaviour that needs scripting. Because `{js}` cells and Mermaid both
render in the browser, a `--bare` build empties them (and warns once per kind) rather
than shipping a broken placeholder, so reach for `--bare` on prose, math, and
server-executed figures, not on pages built around live `{js}` or diagrams.

## Portable, offline builds (email the whole thing as a zip)

A `--out <dir>` build (single doc, site, or book) is a **self-contained folder**: open
`index.html` straight from disk (`file://`), or zip the folder and send it. Everything
the reader needs is copied in and works with no server and no network, **offline**:
navigation and prev/next, the Cmd-K search across the whole book, all math (rendered to
KaTeX at build time with the fonts inlined), the reading enhancers (theme toggle,
reading progress and resume, the mobile pull-up table of contents), and any `{js}` cells (the
`d3` / `Plot` runtimes are vendored, not fetched). Click-to-source is *not* shipped into
a build (it belongs to the live preview), so a reader never gets a dead outline.

**Mermaid diagrams are offline too**, which is worth stating because it is the one part
that looks like it should not be. A page with a `{mermaid}` block gets the ~2.5 MB Mermaid
runtime **inlined into the built page itself**, and only into the pages that actually
contain a diagram. Nothing is fetched at view time. (The live preview serves the same
vendored copy from a same-origin route instead of inlining it, so the dev loop is offline
without adding 2.5 MB to every page it re-serves.)

## Publishing & sharing

A `--out <dir>` folder (a single doc, a site, or a book) is **plain static HTML** — no
server runtime, no build step to reproduce, no database. So *any* way of serving static
files publishes it. Pick whichever fits how you already work:

- **Send it directly.** Zip the folder and email it, or drop it in a shared drive. The
  recipient opens `index.html` from disk — everything works offline (see above). Best for
  a draft to a supervisor or a co-author who just needs to read it.
- **GitHub Pages** (free, good for a book/site tied to a repo). Build into a folder, then
  publish that folder to a `gh-pages` branch (or commit it to `/docs` on your default
  branch) and enable Pages in the repo settings:

  ```sh
  taliesin build mybook --out _site       # build the static site/book
  cd _site
  git init -q && git add -A && git commit -qm "publish"
  git push -f git@github.com:you/mybook.git HEAD:gh-pages   # a throwaway history is fine
  ```

  (Add a `.nojekyll` file to the folder so GitHub serves the `_`-prefixed asset dirs.)
- **A drag-and-drop host** (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel, Surge). Point it at the
  `--out` folder as the publish directory, or drag the folder into their web uploader.
  Nothing to configure — it's already built.
- **Your own server / object storage.** `rsync -a _site/ you@host:/var/www/mybook/`, or
  `aws s3 sync _site s3://your-bucket` behind a CDN. It's just files.

**Set `url:` in `_site.yml` before publishing to a real domain.** The absolute-URL
`<meta>` tags (Open Graph / Twitter card image, canonical link) are only emitted when a
`url:` is configured — `taliesin check` warns if you set a social `image:` without one.
Run `taliesin check <dir>` first: a green check means no broken links, missing anchors, or
other publish-blocking mistakes ship to your readers.

### `taliesin publish`: one command to a passcode-gated URL

For a private draft you want a supervisor or co-author to read at a stable URL,
`taliesin publish <dir>` builds the site or book, gates it behind a shared passcode by
default, and deploys it to [Cloudflare Pages](https://pages.cloudflare.com/) (free, works
with a private repo). The passcode is stored as a Cloudflare secret, never in your repo,
and the flow is one-way: publish never writes back to your source. The build is also
strict by default, so a broken cross-reference or a failed code cell fails the deploy
instead of shipping; pass `--no-strict` to deploy anyway.

One-time setup per project (needs [Wrangler](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/),
Cloudflare's CLI, and a Cloudflare API token with the Pages:Edit permission):

```sh
export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=...      # also CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
wrangler pages project create my-book --production-branch production
wrangler pages secret put PASSWORD --project-name my-book   # type the passcode once
```

Then, every time you want to update the live copy:

```sh
taliesin publish .                   # build, gate, deploy; prints https://my-book.pages.dev
taliesin publish . --dry-run         # build + gate + print the deploy command, no deploy
taliesin publish . --public          # build + deploy WITHOUT the passcode gate (public URL)
```

The Cloudflare project name defaults to a slug of the directory name; override it with
`--project-name` or a `publish:` block in `_site.yml`:

```yaml
publish:
  provider: cloudflare
  project: my-book
```

Add `gate: false` to that block (or pass `--public` on the command line) to deploy a
public, un-gated site instead; either way `publish` prints a loud warning so a public
deploy is never silent.

Your readers open the URL, type the passcode once in the browser's password prompt (no
account needed), and see the current build. A shared passcode keeps casual strangers out;
it is forwardable and is not per-person access control, which is the right level for a
work-in-progress draft to a supervisor.

## Starting from something: `init` and `new`

`init` scaffolds a whole project: `_site.yml`, a hello-world `index.tmd`, an `AGENTS.md`
briefing for coding agents, and a `.taliesin/` directory holding the JSON schemas your
editor completes `_site.yml` and front matter from. `--template site` adds an `about.tmd`;
`--template book` adds the chapter files. `new` scaffolds a single document inside one:

```sh
taliesin new post my-first-post   # posts/my-first-post/index.tmd, dated today
taliesin new page about           # about.tmd
taliesin new deck my-talk         # my-talk.tmd, with `format: deck`
```

The scaffold is correct on its first save: it renders, and `taliesin check` passes on it
with no diagnostics. That is not a promise, it is a test. What each `new` writes is pinned
byte-for-byte by `corpus/scaffold/`, which the corpus regression net renders and lints like
any other document, so a front-matter key the validator would reject cannot survive in the
scaffold. A slug must be lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (it becomes the page's URL),
and like `init`, `new` refuses to overwrite an existing file.

## Inspecting a document: `render`, `blocks`, and `symbols`

Three commands print to a terminal instead of starting a server, which is handy for
piping, diffing, and debugging.

`render` writes one complete, self-contained HTML page to **stdout**. It is the
non-interactive twin of a single-file `build`, so you can redirect or pipe it:

```sh
taliesin render post.tmd > post.html
taliesin render post.tmd | grep -c data-block-id   # count emitted blocks
```

Unlike `build` and `preview`, `render` **never starts a kernel**: `{python}` and
`{r}` cells emit as highlighted source with an empty output block (no plots, and any
`@fig-` reference to a computed figure dangles), so it warns when it sees kernel cells:

```text
warn: render does not execute code cells (2 kernel cells emitted as source;
      figures/outputs will be empty). Use `build` or `preview` to run them.
```

`{js}` cells are fine, since they run in the browser. Use `build`/`preview` when you
need the executed outputs.

`blocks` is a debug view of the block model: for each block it prints the
content-hash id, the `line:col-line:col` sourcepos, the origin file (`(primary)` or
an `{{< include >}}`d path), and a 64-character text preview of the rendered HTML.
It does not execute anything. The shape is one aligned row per block:

```text
title: Some("Hello")
3 block(s)

id                sourcepos       source-file             preview
a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8  1:1-1:14        (primary)               Hello
0f9e8d7c6b5a4039  3:1-5:32        (primary)               A short paragraph that introduces…
7c6b5a4039f0e1d2  7:1-9:3         _includes/note.tmd      Note: included from another file
```

This is the same `data-block-id` + `data-sourcepos` that drive click-to-source and
incremental updates, so `blocks` is the way to confirm a block carries the source map
you expect (and which file it came from) without opening the browser dev tools.

`symbols` lists every cross-reference target the document defines: the anchors you can
write after `@`, each with the number Taliesin resolved for it.

```text
$ taliesin symbols post.tmd
fig-scree                     fig    1
sec-why                       sec    1
tbl-summary                   tbl    1
```

The point is that it sees **both** ways an anchor can be written: the brace form
(`## Why it works {#sec-why}`) and the cell form (`#| label: fig-scree`). A regex over
the source can only find the first, which is why the VS Code companion asks the binary
instead of guessing: `--format json` is what feeds its `@`-completion.

```sh
taliesin symbols post.tmd --format json | jq '.[].id'
```

Like `render` and `blocks`, it renders in memory and **never starts a kernel**, so an
editor can call it while you type. A cell's `label:` is registered when the block model
is built, long before the cell would run.

`--host` binds `0.0.0.0`, so it gates non-loopback access behind a token baked into
the printed LAN URL and QR (`?t=…`, then a `tali_token` cookie); a fresh token is
minted each server start, and loopback (`127.0.0.1`) is exempt. See
[Reading on any screen](../using/preview.tmd) for the full behaviour.

Put `target/release/taliesin` on your `PATH` (a symlink, or a small wrapper script
that rebuilds it when the tool's sources change) so `taliesin preview <file>`
works from anywhere.

## Machine-facing commands: `read`, `map`, `vocab`, `mcp`, `lsp`

A second family of commands exists for *programs* rather than a human reader: an AI
agent driving the write-check-read loop, an editor asking for autocomplete data, a
CI job. They are all offline, and none of them writes to your source: the `.tmd`
stays the single editing surface (the preview is a read-only view of it, and so are
these). [Working with an agent](../using/agents.tmd) walks through the loop; this is
the per-command reference.

`read` projects the rendered document to structured **plain text**: headings, the
resolved `Figure N` / cross-reference numbers, callouts, fenced code, and math as
raw TeX. It is what an agent "sees" without a browser or any HTML, a *view* of the
document, not a new output format. Static by default (like `render`, it does not run
code cells), so kernel cells show as source:

```sh
taliesin read post.tmd                 # the plain-text projection
taliesin read post.tmd --run           # also execute cells, report what each produced
taliesin read post.tmd --run --format json | jq   # structured per-cell results
```

`--run` executes `{python}`/`{r}` cells and, when a local headless Chrome is
available, observes each `{js}` chart (`[js: produced, <svg W×H>]`) and reports what
every cell produced, so an agent can confirm a figure actually rendered.

The `{js}` half needs two things, and reports `[js: skipped, …]` with the reason when
either is missing. It needs a **system Chrome** at runtime (Taliesin never downloads a
browser), and it needs the binary to have been **built with the `headless-js` feature**.
That feature is off by default because its browser driver is roughly a third of the whole
build; the released binaries carry it, so a downloaded Taliesin is the complete tool, and
a source build opts in with one cargo flag (the project README gives the exact command).
Everything else about `read --run` is unaffected either way.

`map` is the whole-project outline in one read-only call: every page in nav / chapter
order (its path, URL, title, date, categories, layout), the nav and any `mounts:`, the
cross-reference graph (each anchor, where it is defined, and its backlinks), and any
embedded decks. It reuses site discovery, so it never starts a kernel, and it is how
an agent plans an edit across a site without opening each file:

```sh
taliesin map . --format json | jq '.pages[].url'
```

Each page also carries `words` (its prose length) and `headings` (its outline, each
heading carrying the number the rendered page shows), so an agent can judge a page's
size and shape without reading it.

`skim` projects a whole book down to the layers a reader actually skims, as one
linear stream: numbered headings, each section's **opening sentence**, and the
captions, callout titles and theorem statements that carry meaning on their own. Read
top to bottom, it answers the question a table of contents cannot, namely whether the
book still makes sense when nobody reads the paragraphs:

```sh
taliesin skim .                 # the linear stream
taliesin skim . --format json   # the same projection, machine-shaped
```

Two rules keep it honest. It always prints the **raw** opening sentence, never a
rewrite or a summary; and where it has a judgement to offer, it says so beside the
text (`(no opening prose)`) rather than hiding the row. A section that is genuinely
thin and a section the projection simply missed must never look the same, or the
thing stops being a measurement.

It reads the section's first *paragraph*, so fenced code, tab panels, and the
contents of a callout or theorem box are not mistaken for the author's prose. Like
`map`, it reuses site discovery and never starts a kernel.

`vocab` emits the editor autocomplete vocabulary as one JSON blob (front-matter keys,
cell options, callout and theorem kinds, div classes, cross-reference prefixes). It is
generated from the validator's own lists, so it never drifts from what `check`
enforces, and it is what feeds the VS Code companion's completions.

`mcp` runs a local, offline **stdio MCP server** (Model Context Protocol) so an MCP
host can drive the read / validate / build loop without shelling out. It exposes six
tools, `check`, `read`, `symbols`, `map`, `vocab`, and `build`, and deliberately **no
write, edit, or preview tool**, so the `.tmd` stays your direct edit surface.

::: {.callout-warning}
## The MCP server is not a sandbox

The no-write guarantee is about the `.tmd` edit surface, not containment. There is no
project root: every tool reads any path you hand it (including outside the project),
and `build` writes HTML beside that path and executes the document's code cells.
Contain it with the host's own sandbox and working directory.
:::

`lsp` runs a local, offline **stdio LSP server** (Language Server Protocol) so any LSP
editor (Neovim, Helix, Zed, VS Code) gets live `.tmd` diagnostics as you type, the same
validators as `check`, run on the unsaved buffer. It is parse-only: no kernel, no code
execution, and it never edits your source.

```
-- Neovim (nvim-lspconfig or vim.lsp.start)
cmd = { "taliesin", "lsp" }
```

## Shell completions

`taliesin completions <bash|zsh|fish|powershell>` prints a completion script to stdout.
The script is a thin shim that asks the running binary for candidates, so Tab completes
subcommands, per-command flags (with descriptions), and, for a path argument, only
`.tmd` files plus directories that contain one (site and book roots first). It registers
both `taliesin` and the `tali` alias, and all the logic lives in one place in the binary,
so no per-shell list can drift.

See [Shell completion](shell-completion.tmd) for the one-line install per shell.

## Environment

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `TALIESIN_PYTHON` | Python with `ipykernel` for `{python}` cells (default `python3`) |
| `TALIESIN_R` | R with `IRkernel` for `{r}` cells (default `R`) |
| `TALIESIN_CELL_TIMEOUT` | Per-cell wall-clock cap in seconds before interrupt (default 120; `0` disables the cap, but a per-output silent-hang backstop still applies) |
| `TALIESIN_RENDER_TIMEOUT` | Per-render wall-clock cap in seconds (default 30; `0` disables it). Rendering is not execution: it never legitimately takes seconds, so this is a watchdog against a pathological document, not a budget to tune. A render that exceeds it is abandoned with a located diagnostic instead of hanging the preview |
| `TALIESIN_JS_TIMEOUT` | How long `read --run` waits for a page's `{js}` cells to settle in headless Chrome, in seconds (default 10). Separate from `TALIESIN_CELL_TIMEOUT` so a long kernel budget doesn't also stretch the browser wait |
| `TALIESIN_MATH_IMAGE_TIMEOUT` | How long `taliesin lsp` waits for one math hover to rasterize in headless Chrome, in seconds (default 10). A hover is a glance, so exceeding this is not an error: the hover falls back to the Unicode preview, exactly as it does on a machine with no Chrome at all |
| `TALIESIN_HOST` | Same as `--host` (expose on the LAN) |
| `TALIESIN_OPEN` | Same as `--open` (launch a browser) |
| `TALIESIN_NO_EXEC` | Same as `--no-exec` (never execute code cells) |
| `TALIESIN_NO_CACHE` | Ignore and skip writing the `_freeze/` execution cache |
| `TALIESIN_NO_CLEAR` | Don't clear the terminal on each render (keep scrollback) |
| `TALIESIN_MERMAID_URL` | Point the Mermaid loader at a different copy of the library. Builds and previews are already offline without it (inlined and same-origin respectively), so this is for pinning a specific Mermaid version or serving your own copy; on load failure a visible `[data-mermaid-error]` banner shows the diagram source |

Without a kernel, cells render as highlighted source and the preview shows a
"kernel unavailable" diagnostic rather than failing.
